17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510601} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3723d1-3bbb-5378-bdf1-8adae692d227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510604} {:answer/id #uuid "c6097b50-9415-5938-9802-60334296d427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510615} {:answer/id #uuid "f5822848-cd15-5ab7-809a-95aee17f1031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5822848-cd15-5ab7-809a-95aee17f1031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510620} :answer-field/id #uuid "61a929df-3a8c-52c0-bc0d-8ef7863835ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c979d384-64a3-52ee-ad97-dd8b3f818ebf" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}-13 & 11 \\\\ 12 & -12\\end{vmatrix}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-13 & 11 \\\\ 12 & -12\\end{vmatrix} & =-13\\cdot (-12)-11\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =156-132 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511457 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fe86c01-fa33-54c0-919e-aaad045de4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511016} {:answer/id #uuid "91cc57eb-09e7-59eb-aa0f-280a8c492891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511083} {:answer/id #uuid "94960e82-187d-589c-a1c2-ed096c94c497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511087} {:answer/id #uuid "bbaa8abc-d95e-51ca-a4d1-7a1aa516b455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511168} {:answer/id #uuid "f46a71c6-0e54-5d62-9732-93eefd0150bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbaa8abc-d95e-51ca-a4d1-7a1aa516b455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511168} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdfd2c93-cc42-5e18-b039-6099f904aa29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "053eddb0-5c93-560e-b92c-a89951cbd49c" :question/math-academy-id "q-42239" :question/problem "![](images/93/938f91220033a2174c011c1f8d65ff61e81e79b8af0ef718411169364c885418.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=t\\,i+\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{t}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0]-[-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02757ddb-6720-5404-868b-7b1166fb2c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510781} {:answer/id #uuid "083e8245-b859-5f0f-b493-6e1eb22d754b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510797} {:answer/id #uuid "1e791730-85c4-5ff7-ad54-6e399657153c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510851} {:answer/id #uuid "3c70ca77-ec27-57f8-b078-51030fa03414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510920} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d4bd2c-dd7d-5ebf-8ab9-0d013cef2b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2d4bd2c-dd7d-5ebf-8ab9-0d013cef2b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511284} :answer-field/id #uuid "1557ad1a-a45b-592a-8609-24271802b200" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ae04e3b-017d-534f-b76c-ac600584e435" :question/math-academy-id "q-126568" :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\nI. $P(x,y)=-y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\n\nII. $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=y$\n\nIII. $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=6{y}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511542 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3242ed2a-ee0f-5b6e-92a0-da63b077c141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510888} {:answer/id #uuid "482ab86f-4fe2-571e-b0ca-93e2bd5d4903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510943} {:answer/id #uuid "a3031b19-d41c-5adf-a1c1-fb61f1fe36c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511124} {:answer/id #uuid "bb43d5ac-3266-5668-89fa-191462493dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511167} {:answer/id #uuid "e19c04bb-3f86-514f-ad0d-22da5d1ba873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb43d5ac-3266-5668-89fa-191462493dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511167} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b42e264-51ff-5675-8ac9-a399601959a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad2198cd-84ab-52d9-a653-128de0924655" :question/math-academy-id "q-126754" :question/problem "![](images/86/86772e27f0a3226a608a1188fd8f58b9a8956f5f02fdbc5f9c2d29e2cef2a54c.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,dx & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡2t(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-18{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =6({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65573630-2003-5f37-a3a0-d25c98f4401a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510996} {:answer/id #uuid "6e780ffa-2a00-5af3-b5f3-92f795812999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511012} {:answer/id #uuid "9dbf5317-be7f-5ed0-a334-2b540864d0af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511112} {:answer/id #uuid "a5484851-2c51-5273-8db2-309d04de9b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511130} {:answer/id #uuid "d89e0626-3b35-5c77-81ab-f0b1350e2a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65573630-2003-5f37-a3a0-d25c98f4401a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510996} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2eb735b-b5bd-5b47-b78f-f9e6cc0794cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdacb1ae-ddf6-5106-9485-7c1e14bd18a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-42297" :question/problem "![](images/35/35b8ad6fd40daf95ba4f77288321d6a570e2df2944d056b9445e9ba4e36f97b7.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06eef846-65a9-5990-acd0-b045e13d1cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{x}^{3/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513548} {:answer/id #uuid "393f1a37-77c6-5bbd-a580-db9adc219b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{-1/3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513680} {:answer/id #uuid "68568d39-43ec-5afd-b396-0468db1f01fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{-1/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513812} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ca349d-23a9-53ce-9e6b-be88f7c8ec44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{1/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513996} {:answer/id #uuid "c14ac74e-1927-56c7-a26e-5732f3c912ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{-3/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68568d39-43ec-5afd-b396-0468db1f01fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{-1/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513812} :answer-field/id #uuid "eae9e030-db6a-548d-840a-748c2245c7ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28f4ccc6-88e6-5461-934d-fc36db9c8ad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-29993" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}$ and that ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}={x}^{-3/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-1/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09e343d0-9e3d-57b4-a7bc-fc9b2686c5b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513556} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2855e0-fb0a-5301-b062-4ac8a822906c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513749} {:answer/id #uuid "81cf656b-bd3c-5484-968b-6e7bc93c98ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513870} {:answer/id #uuid "b4fe22da-80f5-5f8c-9080-2147a668ed55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{1/2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514005} {:answer/id #uuid "dec7850d-5fd9-5c84-b294-33569ce40c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{1/2}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09e343d0-9e3d-57b4-a7bc-fc9b2686c5b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513556} :answer-field/id #uuid "42f6efb1-acc1-594f-9863-9843b604f60e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34621fcb-1a0d-5d3e-9880-09e09830a08f" :question/math-academy-id "q-2024" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt{x}={x}^{1/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{1/2+1}}{(\\frac{1}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2}}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{3/2}}{3}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{x}^{3/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "877168ac-4c34-57ba-a846-5362369f5757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{5}{x}^{3/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513885} {:answer/id #uuid "943d70e7-e52b-597e-9871-44857a8a7ae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}{x}^{5/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513915} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b24cd8-5808-5ff4-8dd5-6d0e70ccaa9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{5}{x}^{5/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514052} {:answer/id #uuid "e25dd75d-4458-58ea-a630-3ccbc3fecb7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}{x}^{3/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514125} {:answer/id #uuid "e355f008-e331-5495-83d3-a7df50fe8e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e355f008-e331-5495-83d3-a7df50fe8e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}{x}^{5/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514130} :answer-field/id #uuid "15bc8d9e-bde6-57af-844a-0b82646dbe35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37cc1eff-0240-5120-ba5e-14ada8bc6fca" :question/math-academy-id "q-29665" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sqrt{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}={x}^{3/2}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/2+1}}{(\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2}}{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{5/2}}{5}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{5/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{3/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25425771-e7b7-59e7-a21c-34da79a147e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{x}^{1/2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513636} {:answer/id #uuid "46f4c2a1-30c4-58bc-a872-acf707c7f214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{x}^{5/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513723} {:answer/id #uuid "bf5da9ff-937c-53a4-b1fa-cc6b757fbfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{x}^{1/2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514037} {:answer/id #uuid "dae23439-686a-536f-8883-85442f6a6ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{1/2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514104} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7a9df8-4ded-54ff-9dd7-df5316a89579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2{x}^{5/2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25425771-e7b7-59e7-a21c-34da79a147e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{x}^{1/2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513636} :answer-field/id #uuid "a255f30f-2d4d-5a18-8010-7066f9da2d12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ce88619-593c-543d-be7b-78867655bb11" :question/math-academy-id "q-113703" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}$ and that ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}={x}^{-3/2}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/2}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-1/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{1/2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
